### 🧠 Query 6: Fuzzy Search with Exact Filter and Date Range

This query uses a `bool` query to combine fuzzy and exact logic:

- `match` on `test_case_name` with `"fuzziness": "AUTO"` allows forgiving searches (e.g., "Updte" will still match "Update")
- `term` on `review_status` ensures only documents marked `unreviewed` are included (exact match on a `keyword` field)
- `range` filter on `created` includes only documents created in 2023 or later
- Results are sorted by `created` date in descending order

This is great for searching user-input terms while still enforcing strict filters.

# Define advanced query body separately for clarity
query_body = {
    "query": {
        "bool": {
            "must": [
                {  # Fuzzy full-text match on test_case_name (slightly misspelled input OK)
                    "match": {
                        "test_case_name": {
                            "query": "Mass Updte",
                            "fuzziness": "AUTO"
                        }
                    }
                },
                {  # Exact match on review_status using keyword type
                    "term": {
                        "review_status": {
                            "value": "unreviewed"
                        }
                    }
                }
            ],
            "filter": [
                {  # Only include docs created after 2023-01-01
                    "range": {
                        "created": {
                            "gte": "2023-01-01"
                        }
                    }
                }
            ]
        }
    },
    "size": 5,
    "sort": [
        {"created": {"order": "desc"}}
    ]
}

# Execute query
response = os_client.search(index="hackaton_write", body=query_body)
response["hits"]["hits"]

### 📊 Query 7: Aggregate AI Models with Average Accuracy

This aggregation query analyzes your index by:

- Grouping documents by `ai_model` (a keyword field)
- Counting how many documents used each model
- Calculating the average `accuracy` score per model

This is useful for evaluating model performance trends, doing QA audits, or analyzing experimental rollouts.  
You can easily replace `accuracy` with `actionability`, `clarity_sequence`, or other fields for broader evaluation.

query_body = {
    "size": 0,  # No documents, just aggregations
    "aggs": {
        "models": {
            "terms": {
                "field": "ai_model",
                "size": 10  # Top 10 models
            },
            "aggs": {
                "avg_accuracy": {
                    "avg": {
                        "field": "accuracy"
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

response = os_client.search(index="hackaton_write", body=query_body)
response["aggregations"]
